{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of upward momentum: When recent history shows a sharp price spike (e.g., >3 EUR in 2 days) and sentiment is positive, the model's linear extrapolation fails to capture the peak magnitude; apply a +3 to +5 EUR upward adjustment to the first 5-7 days of the forecast.",
    "Apply aggressive mean reversion after peaks: The model consistently fails to anticipate the speed and depth of the subsequent crash following a spike. Reduce the forecast by 10-15% starting from day 6 onwards to account for the rapid drop from ~80 EUR to ~63 EUR.",
    "Invert sentiment weighting for short-term volatility: High positive sentiment (1.0) in the history often precedes a local top rather than a sustained rally in this dataset. Treat strong positive sentiment signals as contrarian indicators for the immediate 3-5 day horizon, expecting a pullback rather than a continuation.",
    "Reduce long-horizon drift: The model exhibits a persistent upward drift (or flat stability) that ignores the mean-reverting nature of the market post-spike. Flatten the forecast curve significantly after the first week, anchoring it closer to the recent historical mean (~68-70 EUR) rather than the peak levels.",
    "Adjust for lag in trend reversal: The model reacts too slowly to the shift from bullish to bearish trends. If the 3-day moving average of the history turns down while sentiment remains high, immediately truncate the forecast growth and introduce a downward slope."
  ]
}
